# Emacs Lisp Design Guidelines for Long-Term Compatibility

## 1. Core Principles

Three priorities settle a design question, in this order: **Compatibility**,
**Safety**, **Efficiency**. This is a priority order for when requirements
compete, not an equally weighted checklist.

-   **Compatibility comes first, and it means two things:** keeping normal,
    intended, existing behavior and workflows working, and staying
    compatible with the GNU Emacs versions this repository currently
    supports (23.4 and later). It does not mean preserving whatever the code
    currently does, unconditionally, forever.
    -   A clear bug or a regression is not preserved as though it were
        historical behavior.
    -   Being obsolete or deprecated is not, by itself, a reason to remove a
        legacy component, and neither is the mere existence of a built-in or
        native replacement.
    -   Before retiring anything, its user-visible behavior and its
        dependents are checked, and retirement is a deliberate decision made
        on that evidence, not an inference from age or from a warning.
    -   Where intended behavior is unclear, history, the documented
        interface, and maintenance intent settle it together with the
        implementation; the implementation alone, when it shows a
        regression, is not treated as the new specification on that basis.
    -   Raising the GNU Emacs 23.4 floor, and any other decision to retire a
        support commitment outright, is for the maintainer to decide
        explicitly.
-   **Safety comes second.** A change that is faster or smaller is not taken
    if it weakens safety, and code kept for Compatibility is not itself
    unsafe merely for being old.
-   **Efficiency comes third.** It matters, but a startup-time gain that
    breaks a normal workflow, drops support within the supported version
    range, or weakens safety is not worth taking.

-   **Retire legacy components deliberately, not by inference:**\
    Being obsolete, deprecated, or legacy is not by itself a reason to
    remove a component, and neither is the existence of a built-in or
    native replacement. Code that is actually loaded must not be
    classified as unused, and a dependency must not be called orphaned
    merely because its caller was removed first; check the full
    dependency graph before retiring anything. Before retiring a
    component, enumerate its user-visible behavior; when replacing it,
    confirm the replacement is behaviorally equivalent, and keep any
    historical behavior whose equivalence cannot be confirmed. A
    cleanup or modernization that changes observable behavior is not a
    plain refactoring, and in long-lived configuration, proven behavior
    takes priority over code age.

-   **Detect capabilities where they genuinely vary, not by reflex:**\
    Guard a call with `fboundp`, `boundp`, or `featurep` where API
    availability actually differs across the supported Emacs range, where an
    optional package's presence is not guaranteed, where build, platform, or
    environment affects what exists, or where the target is a conditionally
    defined function or variable. A standard API guaranteed across the whole
    supported range, or a project-owned function that load order already
    guarantees is defined, does not need a mechanical existence check merely
    for its own sake. Where capability detection is genuinely the more
    appropriate tool, prefer it to a version number; where a loader is
    switching between feature sets that one specific Emacs version
    introduced wholesale, a version check is the clearer tool there and is
    not itself a violation of this principle.

-   **Welcome new capabilities as version-gated enhancements:**\
    A minimal, stable core keeps GNU Emacs 23.4 and every supported version
    after it working normally. That does not require every new capability to
    also run on 23.4: a feature available only from some newer Emacs version
    may be enabled only on that version and cleanly bypassed on older ones,
    and does not need to be reimplemented for versions that predate it.
    Using a new, version-specific capability is not a compatibility
    violation by itself; breaking an existing workflow on a version that is
    still supported is.

-   **Prefer compatibility layers over rewrites:**\
    When upstream APIs change, introduce compatibility bridges rather
    than rewriting historical configuration or bundled third-party
    code.

-   **Avoid new internal API dependencies:**\
    Project-owned code must not introduce new dependencies on
    `--private` functions or undocumented variables. Existing internal
    API dependencies in historical bundled third-party code may be
    retained when they are required for compatibility with older GNU
    Emacs versions; where a stable built-in replacement exists on newer
    versions, contain the historical path behind a compatibility or
    capability check instead of rewriting it unnecessarily.

-   **Prioritize longevity over trend adoption:**\
    Avoid short-lived frameworks or experimental APIs unless they can
    degrade gracefully.

-   **Match wording strength to what a rule actually requires:**\
    Reserve absolute wording -- `must`, `always`, `never` -- for an
    invariant that admits no reasonable exception. Write a design
    preference or a situational rule with wording that shows its actual
    weight, such as `prefer`, `should`, or `when appropriate`. Word a rule
    so that applying it literally does not produce a result that works
    against Compatibility, Safety, or Efficiency. Where a rule's wording
    and its purpose conflict, resolve it by the Core Principles above and
    the intended behavior, not by the literal wording alone. This does not
    introduce a formal MUST/SHOULD/MAY taxonomy; it only asks that the
    wording already used here match its actual strength.

-   **Change established infrastructure only by explicit decision:**\
    Finding a possible safety, compatibility, maintainability, cleanup, or
    refactoring improvement does not by itself authorize an implementation
    change. Documentation work, guideline work, review, diagnostics, cleanup,
    or another task whose stated scope does not include an implementation
    change must not expand into one merely because an improvement opportunity
    is discovered.

    A safety-motivated change is not automatically justified by Safety having
    priority over Efficiency. Added complexity can itself create new failure
    modes, compatibility risk, operational risk, and maintenance cost.
    Evaluate the proposed change as a whole.

    Before changing established behavior, weigh the probability and impact of
    the failure being prevented against the added complexity, new failure
    modes, compatibility risk, operational cost, maintenance cost, and
    rollback cost.

    Behavior that has operated reliably over time is evidence and has value of
    its own. Do not refactor long-lived configuration or compatibility code
    merely because another design appears cleaner, more modern, or more
    defensive.

    An implementation change is made only when that change has been explicitly
    chosen as part of the work being performed. Before deployment, validate it
    in the supported and representative GNU Emacs environments affected by
    the change. A successful check on one convenient GNU Emacs version is not
    sufficient evidence for a change whose compatibility risk extends to
    other supported versions.

    Scope discipline does not mean leaving documentation inconsistent with an
    implementation change that is already authorized. When an implementation
    change alters behavior described by a project-owned header, FEATURES,
    README, compatibility documentation, or release history, update the
    directly affected documentation as part of the same change unless the
    stated scope explicitly excludes documentation changes. Do not use that
    requirement as permission for unrelated cleanup in the same file or
    repository.

