## 9. Dependency Policy

External dependencies must never compromise stability.

Rules:

-   Prefer a built-in package whenever it is enough on its own to meet the
    need.
-   External packages are not excluded on principle: adopt one when it
    supplies clear functional value a built-in does not, and be cautious
    about a hard dependency on a rapidly changing package.
-   Optional packages must degrade gracefully if absent.

Absence of an optional package should **reduce functionality, not break
startup.** Errors raised while evaluating an installed package should
remain visible rather than being silently suppressed.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 10. Compatibility Layer Strategy

When upstream changes occur, prefer a compatibility bridge when the bridge
can reach the problem.

Examples:

    core-compat-bridge.el
    cl-compat-bridge.el

Compatibility layers allow:

-   preservation of historical configuration
-   isolation of API transitions
-   incremental modernization

This strategy avoids large-scale rewrites and preserves long-term
maintainability.

The bundled files under `emacs.d/elisp/3rd-party/` are historical
third-party code, kept as close to the upstream text as possible so that
it stays recognisable and a later copy can replace them. A bridge is the
default place for a fix reaching the problem, and only when the bridge
cannot reach it may a documented minimal compatibility fix be applied to
the bundled source instead. Large-scale rewrites of bundled source are
not done.

What a bridge can reach:

-   A bridge is loaded into the same session as the code it serves, so
    it can repair whatever lives in state that the session keeps:
    function cells and aliases, symbol properties such as the
    obsolescence mark a deprecated alias carries, variables, features,
    hooks, and `load-path`.
-   A bridge cannot reach state that is created and discarded inside one
    compilation. `byte-compile-function-environment` is bound afresh for
    every compilation and `byte-compile-unresolved-functions` is reset
    for every file, so `declare-function` counts only inside the file
    that makes the call. A declaration for a conditionally defined or
    optional function has to sit in that file.
-   A bridge cannot reach a defect in the text of the bundled file
    itself. A call written wrongly, such as a `case` clause whose key is
    quoted, is in the file, and only the file can carry the correction.
-   Decide by that reach, not by the size of the diff. A one line change
    to a bundled file is still a change to a bundled file, and a repair
    in the bridge that removes a hundred reports is still one change to
    our own code.

What a bridge must not do:

-   Do not silence a class of warnings. Suppressing a category, such as
    unresolved function calls, hides the same report everywhere,
    including in our own files, and contradicts section 8. Repair the
    specific names the bridge is responsible for.
-   Do not widen a repair past what the bridge supplies. Clearing an
    obsolescence mark applies to the names the bridge itself bridges,
    and an unrelated deprecation keeps warning.
-   Do not change what a bundled file means. A bridge supplies the names
    the file expects; it does not redefine the file's own macros to give
    its calls a different reading.
-   Do not leave another package's definitions broken. Repair the least
    that achieves the result, so whatever that package alone provides
    keeps working.
-   Guard each repair by the condition that makes it necessary, so it is
    a no-op elsewhere. A bridge runs on every supported Emacs, and a
    repair aimed at a modern deprecation must do nothing on the versions
    that never had it.

When a bundled file must be edited:

-   Establish first that the reach above rules a bridge out, and say so
    in the request, so that the exception is visible.
-   Prefer a compiler-only annotation, such as `declare-function`, which
    expands to nil and adds no run-time behavior.
-   Otherwise confine the change to the expression that is wrong.
-   Restructuring bundled code is the last resort, and is not done to
    remove a report that the compiler merely emits.

